  "type": "3 marks — Explain",
  "marks": "Definition (1 mark): a protocol is a set of agreed rules and standards governing how data is formatted, transmitted and received across a network. Why important (2 marks): without protocols, devices from different manufacturers could not communicate (1 mark); protocols ensure all devices on a network follow the same rules so data is sent and received reliably, regardless of device or operating system (1 mark for a developed explanation). Named example adds quality but is not required for full marks. [3 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "A school is setting up a new computer network in its IT suite. Compare a star topology and a bus topology, identifying one advantage and one disadvantage of each.",
  "type": "4 marks — Compare",
  "marks": "Star topology advantage (1 mark): if one device fails it does not affect the rest of the network / each device has a dedicated connection to the hub so performance is better. Star topology disadvantage (1 mark): if the central hub or switch fails the entire network goes down / requires more cable than a bus topology. Bus topology advantage (1 mark): cheaper to install as less cable is needed / easy to add devices to the backbone. Bus topology disadvantage (1 mark): if the backbone cable fails the entire network fails / performance degrades as more devices are added. [4 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "Explain what packet sniffing is and describe one way an organisation can protect its data from this threat.",
  "type": "4 marks — Explain and Describe",
  "marks": "Packet sniffing explanation (2 marks): software or hardware that intercepts and reads data packets as they travel across a network (1); on an unencrypted network the attacker can read sensitive information such as usernames, passwords and financial data in plain text (1). Protection method (2 marks): using encryption such as HTTPS / TLS (1) scrambles the data so that intercepted packets cannot be read without the decryption key (1). Other valid methods: VPN, using a secured/encrypted Wi-Fi network. [4 marks]"
 },
 {
  "text": "A rural village has no mobile phone signal and no cable broadband infrastructure. A local business needs reliable internet access to run its online shop.\n\nDiscuss which connectivity method or methods would be most suitable for this business, considering the advantages and disadvantages of each option.",
  "type": "8 marks — Evaluate (Extended Response)",
  "marks": "Mastering (7-8 marks): Identifies satellite communication as the primary viable option given the absence of mobile and cable infrastructure. Advantages of satellite: works anywhere with a clear view of the sky, no reliance on ground infrastructure, can support broadband speeds with modern low-Earth orbit satellites. Disadvantages: higher latency with traditional geostationary satellites (which may affect real-time processes); equipment costs; monthly subscription. Considers whether 3G/4G/5G might be possible if partial signal exists with a booster. Microwave could work if the business has line-of-sight to a tower and the infrastructure is available. Wired Ethernet connects internal devices on site but cannot solve the last-mile connectivity problem. A substantiated recommendation is made (e.g. satellite is the only realistic option in this scenario, and low-Earth orbit satellite services are increasingly viable for rural businesses). Discusses trade-off between performance and cost.\nSecure (5-6 marks): Satellite identified and discussed with advantages and disadvantages; at least one other method considered; recommendation attempted.\nDeveloping (3-4 marks): One or two relevant methods identified but analysis is limited.\nEmerging (1-2 marks): A basic relevant point with no development. [8 marks]"
 }
